### run 5
 denoise -- three-pixel median vs the reference implementation
================================================================================

OpenCV 4.8.0-dev, cv::getNumThreads() = 1; binCV is single-threaded

DENOMINATOR: the reference pipeline's three-pixel median on the SAME
binary content stored as CV_8U -- cv::min/cv::max over two zero-filled
neighbour matrices, ported call for call. That is what the pipeline runs
today without binCV.

binCV rows: the fused one-pass kernel (ops/denoise.hpp) at uint32 and uint64,
and the composed shiftDown + shiftLeft + majority3 spelling it replaced --
same image, three passes, two frame-sized scratch buffers.

TWO OpenCV ROWS. 'OpenCV CV_8U' re-zeroes only the two neighbour matrices,
whose zeros ARE the border; 'OpenCV as-written' also re-zeroes the four
temporaries the reference builds with cv::Mat::zeros and then completely
overwrites. The DENOMINATOR is the first, so every ratio is conservative by
the gap between them.

The checksum column folds EVERY destination pixel and is representation-
independent, so all rows of a size must print the same value.

Working set is one call's live buffers, not a per-buffer ratio (CLAUDE.md,
peak).

FIXED PER-CALL COST (hazard 5), measured on a 2x2 frame -- the part of every
row below that is not the operation:
 OpenCV reference (8 cv:: calls):    0.862 us per call
 binCV denoiseMedian3:    0.002 us per call
 Divided by a frame's pixel count this is what a small frame's ns/pixel is
 mostly made of. It is NOT subtracted from the tables; it is printed beside
 them so the ladder is read with it.

